videos.script:
You are writing a study session into the planner, and the phone rings. {{call}} You answer. Twenty minutes later you come back, and the half-typed session is either there or gone.

Which one depends on a rule most beginners never hear. The app does not decide when it runs. The system does. {{system}} It moves the app between a handful of states: in front and listening, visible but interrupted, in the background, frozen, and gone. {{states}}

Android and iOS give those states different names, and React Native gives them a third set. This lesson maps all three onto one model. {{map}} Then you run that model as a small program, feed it an afternoon with a phone call in it, and watch which callbacks each platform fires. {{run}}

At the end of the afternoon the system kills the app to free memory, and the app is told nothing at all. {{silence}} That silence is the reason for the one rule this lesson leaves you with: save on the way to the background, because there may be no later.
